# Dungeon Builder specimen pack (rpg-project#175) — real output from
# dungeonYaml.ts's own serializeDungeon/stripToV1Subset, committed
# byte-accurate on purpose. Prettier's YAML formatter reformats flow-style
# maps onto multiple lines and swaps double quotes for single, which would
# silently diverge these files from both what the real serializer emits
# and what's posted verbatim on rpg-project#175 — see
# src/author/specimens/README.md for the full context. Moved from
# src/concepts/dungeon-builder/ to src/author/ (rpg-project#194 graduation).
src/author/specimens/*.yaml
src/author/specimens/*.json

# Exact exported World Building forcing specimen (web#951).
src/compositions/fixtures/*.scene.json

# The toolkit's own dungeonspec testdata, copied byte-for-byte
# (rpg-project#368 §3.1 and its predecessor). These files are not this
# repo's to format: they are the exact text the compiler compiles and
# `GetDungeon` serves, and the builder's fixture and the server's fixture
# have to be ONE text or a disagreement about what a dungeon IS can hide
# between them. Prettier's YAML formatter respaces flow sequences
# (`[[0,0],[1,0]]` -> `[[0, 0], [1, 0]]`) and breaks flow maps across
# lines, which is exactly the silent divergence the copy exists to prevent
# — and it had already done it to reference-tomb.yaml before anyone
# noticed, because no test asserted a byte prettier would touch.
src/author/fixtures/*.yaml

# The World Builder's own pinned snapshots, for the same reason as the two
# groups above and with the same failure already observed once: every file here
# is either the toolkit's exact text or this repo's exact emitted bytes, and a
# test asserts the hash. Prettier's YAML formatter respaces flow maps
# (`{weight: 70}` -> `{ weight: 70 }`) and breaks them across lines, which is a
# silent divergence from the file the copy was taken from.
#
# `src/author/fixtures/*.yaml` did not cover this directory, so
# `worldBuilderV4Site.yaml` was reformatted the first time `npm run format` ran
# — caught by its own hash assertion, which is what that assertion is for. This
# pattern also protects `singleRoomV3.golden.yaml`, which was stable only by
# luck: nothing asserted a byte Prettier would have touched.
src/concepts/world-building/fixtures/*.yaml
